Specification AddressCorrection
Description Standardizes US street addresses through the Census Bureau geocoder

# Testing a live API. There are no production classes: the service is the thing
# under test, so the glue is all there is. It builds the query, calls the
# service, lifts the matches out of the response and compares them.
#
# The service is the US Census Bureau's address geocoder, which is public and
# needs no API key:
#
#   https://geocoding.geo.census.gov/geocoder/locations/onelineaddress
#
# It answers with the same data every time, so every expected value below is a
# real one. A cell reading "string" would assert almost nothing -- any non-empty
# text passes -- and the scenario would read as covered while checking that a
# field exists.
#
# The response is nested: {"result": {"addressMatches": [ ... ]}}, and each match
# carries its parts under "addressComponents". A table is flat, so the glue lifts
# result.addressMatches out and flattens each match before comparing. That is
# translation, which is glue's job; no rule about addresses is decided there.

Background
Given base Page is : String
| https://geocoding.geo.census.gov |

# ============================================================
# A complete address comes back standardized
# ============================================================

Scenario Standardize an address that is already complete
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 1600 pennsylvania ave nw, washington, dc |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And match count is : Result Vertical
| MatchCount | 1 |
And the matched address is : Match Vertical
| MatchedAddress  | 1600 PENNSYLVANIA AVE NW, WASHINGTON, DC, 20500 |
| StreetName      | PENNSYLVANIA                                    |
| SuffixType      | AVE                                             |
| SuffixDirection | NW                                              |
| PreDirection    |                                                 |
| City            | WASHINGTON                                      |
| State           | DC                                              |
| Zip             | 20500                                           |

# The caller typed it in lower case and left the ZIP off entirely. Case, and a
# ZIP the caller never supplied, are what the service is for -- so both are
# stated as literals. PreDirection is empty here and the blank cell says so.

# ============================================================
# A spelled out street name becomes its numeral
# ============================================================

Scenario Correct a spelled out ordinal
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 350 fifth ave, new york, ny |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And match count is : Result Vertical
| MatchCount | 1 |
And the matched address is : Match CompareOnly
| MatchedAddress                     | StreetName | Zip   |
| 350 5TH AVE, NEW YORK, NY, 10118   | 5TH        | 10118 |

# "fifth" became "5TH" -- the reason anyone calls an address corrector. This is
# the same rule as "1 Penny LN" becoming "1 Penny Lane", in the direction this
# particular service standardizes.
#
# CompareOnly limits equality to the columns actually shown; every other field
# of Match is filled with ?DNC? and skipped. Use it when the scenario is about
# one rule. A step takes one modifier, so a CompareOnly table cannot also be
# Vertical -- which is why this one is on its side compared with the first.

# ============================================================
# Only the fields the rule is about
# ============================================================

Scenario Keep a directional the caller supplied
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 200 e main st, columbus, oh |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And the matched address is : Match CompareOnly
| MatchedAddress                   | PreDirection |
| 200 E MAIN ST, COLUMBUS, OH, 43215 | E          |

# Adding a field to the response does not break this scenario, because nothing
# but these two columns is compared.

# ============================================================
# Ambiguity returns every candidate
# ============================================================

Scenario Return every candidate for an ambiguous address
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 1600 pennsylvania ave, washington, dc |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And match count is : Result Vertical
| MatchCount | 2 |
And the matched addresses are : Match CompareOnly
| MatchedAddress                                  |
| 1600 PENNSYLVANIA AVE NW, WASHINGTON, DC, 20500 |
| 1600 PENNSYLVANIA AVE SE, WASHINGTON, DC, 20003 |

# Drop the directional and the corrector cannot choose between the two
# Pennsylvania Avenues, so it returns both rather than guessing.
#
# Two rows checked against the array the service returned. The glue matches each
# expected row to an actual one and requires the counts to agree, so the check
# is on the set rather than on the order -- the service happens to be stable
# here, but a test that fails when a service reorders its results is testing the
# wrong thing.

# ============================================================
# An address that does not exist
# ============================================================

Scenario Report an address that cannot be corrected
When sending request : Request Vertical
| Address | 99999 nonexistent rd, nowhere, zz 00000 |
Then response status is : Status Vertical
| Code | 200 |
And match count is : Result Vertical
| MatchCount | 0 |

# A failure to correct is still a 200. The call succeeded; the address did not
# exist. Those are different things, and checking status separately from the
# body is what keeps them apart -- a 404 here would be a defect in the service,
# not an unknown address.

Attributes Request
| Attribute | Type   | Default                            | Notes                                     |
| Method    | String | GET                                |                                           |
| Page      | String | geocoder/locations/onelineaddress  | Appended to the base Page from the Background |
| Address   | String |                                    | As the caller typed it                    |
| Benchmark | String | Public_AR_Current                  | Which edition of the address ranges       |
| Format    | String | json                               |                                           |

# Everything but Address has a default, so a scenario states only what is
# peculiar to it. The glue turns these into the query string.

Attributes Status
| Attribute | Type    | Default | Notes            |
| Code      | Integer | 200     | HTTP status code |

Attributes Result
| Attribute  | Type    | Default | Notes                                |
| MatchCount | Integer |         | How many addresses the service found |

Attributes Match
| Attribute       | Type   | Default | Notes                                  |
| MatchedAddress  | String |         | The standardized address, as one line  |
| PreDirection    | String |         | N, S, E, W before the street name      |
| StreetName      | String |         |                                        |
| SuffixType      | String |         | ST, AVE, RD, LN                        |
| SuffixDirection | String |         | NW, SE and so on, after the suffix     |
| City            | String |         |                                        |
| State           | String |         | Two letters                            |
| Zip             | String |         | Five digits                            |
